+When using the [Array Methods Plugin](./array-methods.md) (`enableArrayMethods()`), callbacks for overridden methods like `filter`, `find`, `some`, and `every` receive **base values** (not drafts). This is the core performance optimization - it avoids creating proxies for every element during iteration.
+
+This holds while nothing in the array has been modified, which is the whole of a read-only pass. Mutating the array moves callbacks onto its current members, which may be drafts, and so does mutating any element in it - a change to an element marks the array itself modified, and the callbacks then have to see that change rather than the base value it replaced. An intervening `filter`, `find` or `slice` does not change what later callbacks receive, even though it creates drafts for the items it returns.
+
+> **A callback argument is your base state, so do not write to it.** Because it is a base value and not a draft, `item.value = 999` inside one of these callbacks mutates the object your state already holds. Immer's guarantee that a producer never touches the base does not cover it, nothing reports the write, and the change is visible outside the producer. Read from callback arguments only, and make changes through the draft - `draft.items[i]` - or through the drafts these methods return.
 
